<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Casbah of Algiers</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kasbah of Algiers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Casbah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> is the old walled town of Algiers, rising on a steep hill above the harbour and crowned by the citadel built in the Ottoman period. Its maze of narrow streets, houses, palaces and mosques dates largely from the time of the Regency, when it was the seat of the deys from 1817. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1992 and was a stronghold of the Algerian War of independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/casbah-of-algiers.jpg" alt="Casbah of Algiers" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: toufik Lerari · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlgerCasbah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Casbah lies at the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and forms its historic core, on a slope with a height difference of 118 metres facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. It has a triangular shape with its base on the Bay of Algiers, so that from the sea it appears as a pyramid or triangular amphitheatre. Its narrow, winding streets, some ending in dead ends or vaulted passages called sabat, are closed to cars, and donkeys have been used for rubbish collection since the Regency. The name comes from the citadel above, the Arabic al-qaṣabah, and originally meant the highest point of the old city before it came to mean the whole medina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A Phoenician port called Ikosim was probably founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> after the late 6th century BCE, and it later became a Roman city, Icosium. The medina itself was built in the 10th century by Bologhin Ibn Ziri of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zirid-dynasty">Zirid dynasty</a> on the ruins of the Roman city, in the territory of the Berber Beni Mezghenna. The name Al-Djaza'ir, the Islets, probably refers to the islands that once faced the port.</p><p>In 1529 the Barbarossa brothers expelled the Spanish from the Peñón islet, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oruc-reis">Aruj Barbarossa</a> joined it to the mainland to make a port, turning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> into the main base for corsairs in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and capital of its regency. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>'s expedition failed in 1541, and the city was then enclosed with a wall and gates such as Bab Azoun and Bab El Oued. The citadel was begun in 1597 and became the seat of the dey's power in 1817.</p><p>The French entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> on 5 July 1830. They demolished much of the lower Casbah, built what is now Place des Martyrs and pulled down the walls. In 1956 leaders of the FLN, among them Abane Ramdane, Larbi Ben M'hidi and Krim Belkacem, settled in the Casbah, which offered hideouts, couriers and clandestinity for urban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare. After independence many native families moved to European-style apartments elsewhere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Casbah has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1992. Its houses, typically 30 to 60 square metres and presenting a single façade, are grouped close together, a pattern thought to date from the Zirid era. Its palaces include Dar Aziza and Dar Hassan Pacha, and its mosques include Jamaa Ketchaoua, Jamaa el Kebir and Jamaa el Jdid. The Thaâlibiyya Madrasa of 1904 is in the neo-Moorish style. Many films were shot there, among them Pépé le Moko (1937) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gillo-pontecorvo">Gillo Pontecorvo</a>'s The Battle of Algiers (1969).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Casbah of Algiers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Casbah of Algiers</b>, now a World Heritage Site, and the Jamaa el Djedid mosque of 1660 survive from the period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
